    "mermaidDiagram": "## 1. Flow Diagram (Mermaid)\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[User sends request to protected Express route] --> B[auth middleware runs<br/>/app/middleware/auth.js]\n    B --> C[Extract JWT from Authorization header/cookie]\n    C --> D[Verify JWT signature using configured secret/key]\n\n    D --> E{Does verifier secret/key<br/>match signing secret/key?}\n\n    E -->|Yes| F[JWT verifies successfully]\n    F --> G[Request continues to route handler]\n    G --> H[User remains authenticated]\n\n    E -->|No| I[Divergence: token signed by different secret/key<br/>or verifier config differs across instance/service]\n    I --> J[jsonwebtoken throws<br/>JsonWebTokenError: invalid signature]\n    J --> K[auth middleware rejects request]\n    K --> L[Request treated as unauthenticated / user appears logged out]\n\n    style F fill:#d4edda,stroke:#28a745,stroke-width:2px\n    style G fill:#d4edda,stroke:#28a745,stroke-width:2px\n    style H fill:#d4edda,stroke:#28a745,stroke-width:2px\n    style I fill:#fff3cd,stroke:#ff9800,stroke-width:2px\n    style L fill:#f8d7da,stroke:#dc3545,stroke-width:2px\n```\n\n## 2. Clarifying Questions\n\n1. Are there multiple app pods/containers/servers handling authenticated requests, and can you compare the effective JWT secret/public key loaded by each one at runtime?\n2. Do Sentry events for `invalid signature` correlate to specific pod IDs, hostnames, deploy versions, or a recent rolling deployment/secret rotation window?\n3. Is JWT issuance performed by the same Node app or by a separate auth service/refresh flow, and do all issuance paths use the exact same algorithm, secret/keypair, and `kid` configuration as `/app/middleware/auth.js` verification?\n4. Can you capture one exact failing token and test that identical token against two different instances to see whether it validates on one instance but fails on another?\n5. On verification failure, what are the decoded JWT header/claims values (`alg`, `kid`, `iss`) and token source (Authorization header vs cookie), and do they differ between successful and failing requests?\n\n## 3. Confidence Score & Breakdown\n\nCONFIDENCE_SCORE: 90\nCONFIDENCE_EVIDENCE: [\"The exception is specifically `JsonWebTokenError: invalid signature`, which strongly indicates a signing/verification secret or key mismatch rather than expiry or authorization logic\", \"The bug is intermittent and described as random logout behavior, which closely matches multi-instance or multi-path configuration inconsistency\", \"The runtime appears containerized (`/app/...`) and the proposed reproduction using different JWT secrets across instances directly matches the observed symptom\"]\nCONFIDENCE_ASSUMPTIONS: [\"The deployment uses multiple instances, services, or auth paths rather than a single static process\", \"The token itself is generally valid and not primarily being corrupted client-side before reaching the middleware\"]\nCONFIDENCE_MISSING: [\"Whether failures correlate to specific instances, deploys, or token issuance paths\", \"A captured failing token plus decoded header/claims and the verifier configuration actually used at failure time\"]\n\n## 4. Severity Classification\n\nSEVERITY: high\nSEVERITY_REASON: This is a user-facing authentication failure in a critical auth component that intermittently invalidates active sessions and can widely disrupt access, though there is no direct evidence yet of data corruption or security compromise.",
